---
/**
 * Masthead (components.md §1): wordmark + 4-item nav. No scroll pinning —
 * long-form reading owns the viewport. The current page item is marked
 * THREE ways — accent border, ink color, weight 800 — so it never depends on
 * color alone (WCAG 1.4.1).
 *
 * Nav targets: /archive/ presets for Reviews/Notes (ui-spec §6), the active
 * year's list, archive and about. Shell pages for these routes are a W5.2
 * obligation (E-112 readiness).
 *
 * ORDER (revised 2026-09-07): Charts · Reviews · Notes · Archive. The row
 * reads in the SAME order the home stacks its sections — Charts, then
 * Latest Reviews, then Notes — so a reader who scrolls the front page and a
 * reader who scans the nav are given the publication in one order rather
 * than two. Charts leads because the annual list is what this site is for;
 * the two formats follow; Archive closes the row as the tool for finding
 * any of them again.
 *
 * ABOUT IS NOT HERE. It moved to the footer: it is the colophon, not a
 * corner to read, and the masthead reads better as a list of things to read.
 *
 * ARTISTS WAS CUT (2026-09-08), REVERSING THE 2026-09-07 PARAGRAPH THIS ONE
 * REPLACES. That paragraph argued Artists had to stay because "reachable
 * from the archive" applies just as much to Reviews and Notes, and cutting
 * on that argument would have emptied the masthead entirely — true then,
 * and still true of Reviews/Notes today, which is exactly why they stay.
 * What changed is /archive/ itself: it is on a path to become the site's
 * SEARCH surface, with Artists as one axis inside it rather than a
 * standalone destination competing with the reading tabs for a masthead
 * slot. A search axis belongs behind the tool that searches, not beside it
 * — the archive is still where you look something up again, Artists is now
 * one of the things you look up, and Reviews/Notes are not axes of the
 * archive but the two things this site actually publishes, which is the
 * distinction this decision draws that the reverted one did not have
 * available yet. AT THE TIME OF THIS EDIT /artists/ (the list) was still a
 * real page and only its masthead slot was being removed; the axis-chip
 * redesign below folded that list INTO the archive chip row and retired the
 * page itself. `/artists/{slug}/`, the per-artist page, was never a masthead
 * item and is unaffected by either change — it stays reachable from a
 * review's own byline (SpecMeta, the hero) and now also from the archive's
 * Artist chips.
 *
 * ARCHIVE BRIEFLY BECAME "SEARCH" (2026-09-09, same day, reverted). /archive/
 * grew a query box and, for a few commits, the masthead label followed that
 * rename. The axis-chip redesign that landed later the same day (see
 * archive/index.astro's intro) folded Artists back in as a fourth chip axis
 * and retired the standalone year/genre/tag pages, which made the page read
 * as the site's browse-and-filter hub again rather than as a search
 * utility bolted onto it — "Archive" names that better than "Search" does,
 * and the decision-maker asked for the old label back explicitly. `key` and
 * `href` were never touched by either rename: both stay 'archive' /
 * '/archive/' regardless of label, so a future rewording never has to touch
 * routing. */
import { getSiteConfig } from '../lib/site.ts';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths.ts';

interface Props {
  /** Which nav item is the current page (aria-current + visual state). */
  current?: 'reviews' | 'stories' | 'list' | 'artists' | 'archive' | 'about';
}

const { current } = Astro.props;
const site = getSiteConfig();

// Every href passes withBase — subpath deploys 404 otherwise (B-1).
const items = [
  { key: 'list', label: 'Charts', href: withBase(`/list/${site.active_year}/`) },
  { key: 'reviews', label: 'Reviews', href: withBase('/archive/reviews/') },
  { key: 'stories', label: 'Notes', href: withBase('/archive/stories/') },
  { key: 'archive', label: 'Archive', href: withBase('/archive/') },
] as const;
---

<header class="masthead">
  <nav aria-label="주 메뉴" class="masthead-inner">
    {/* Wordmark: display 800 + accent period. The period is the one physical
        survivor of the old ink-and-seal identity — the seal shrank to a dot. */}
    <a href={withBase('/')} class="wordmark">{site.site_name}<span class="wordmark-dot" aria-hidden="true">.</span></a>
    <ul class="nav-list">
      {
        items.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <a href={item.href} class:list={['nav-link', { current: current === item.key }]} aria-current={current === item.key ? 'page' : undefined}>
              {item.label}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </nav>
</header>

<style>
  .masthead {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .masthead-inner {
    width: min(var(--shell), 100% - 2 * var(--gutter));
    margin-inline: auto;
    min-height: var(--masthead-h);
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    /* `align-content: center` is NOT decorative here. `align-items: baseline`
       below computes a required row height (max ascent + max descent) that
       is TALLER than `min-height`'s floor once flex-wrap makes this a
       "multi-line-capable" container — left at the default (stretch), the
       single line is stretched flush to the container's cross-start (top)
       instead of centred, which reproduces the exact upward bias this pass
       exists to remove, just one level higher. Verified by removing it:
       the wordmark's box top went to 0 at every width, glued to the top
       rule of .masthead regardless of --masthead-h. */
    align-content: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--s-16);
    flex-wrap: wrap; /* ~640px: two-row wrap allowed, no horizontal scroll */
  }
  .wordmark {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: var(--fs-21);
    line-height: 24px;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    color: var(--ink);
    /* 44px touch target via padding, not layout height */
    padding: var(--s-8) 0;
  }
  .wordmark-dot {
    color: var(--accent-ink);
  }
  /* THE WORDMARK AND THE NAV SHARE A BASELINE, NOT A BOX CENTRE (2026-09-08).
     A 2026-09-07 pass centred the two groups' BOXES (`align-items: center`
     here and on .masthead-inner) and measured them flush to within 0.25px.
     That was the wrong invariant: a reader lines up two pieces of text on a
     shared row by their BASELINES, and "undernote." (lowercase, ascenders,
     no descenders) and "CHARTS · REVIEWS · …" (all caps, no ascenders past
     cap-height, no descenders) are different-shaped ink bands whose centres
     do not sit at the same fraction of their own height. Centring the boxes
     therefore left the wordmark's baseline ~7px below the nav's, which reads
     as the wordmark sagging even though every box was geometrically aligned.
     `align-items: baseline` is the fix, but it only works if the *actual
     glyph* baseline of ".nav-link" reaches ".masthead-inner" — and three
     levels of flex sit between them (.nav-list, the <li>, then .nav-link
     itself is `inline-flex`). Per the flex baseline-propagation algorithm, a
     nested flex container exposes its FIRST CHILD's baseline upward only if
     that child is itself baseline-aligned; anywhere in that chain still set
     to the previous `align-items: center` breaks the chain and the outer
     alignment silently falls back to a synthesized (box-edge) baseline —
     exactly the kind of change that measures as "fine" without being real.
     So `align-items: baseline` is set at EVERY level: here, on the <li>, and
     on .nav-link below. Verified empirically (not just reasoned about) by
     probing the painted glyph baseline — a zero-height inline-block dropped
     at the end of each text run, whose `getBoundingClientRect().bottom` IS
     the baseline — at 6 widths from 480px to 1440px: 0.000px difference at
     all of them (see the a11y spec below, which replaced the box-centre
     assertion this comment used to justify). */
  .nav-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s-24);
  }
  .nav-list li {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .nav-link {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-13);
    line-height: 16px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    min-height: 44px;
    /* WHERE THE LABEL SITS INSIDE ITS 44px TOUCH TARGET (2026-09-08).
       Without this the label sat at the TOP of the box and all 24px of
       touch-target slack hung BELOW the shared baseline. Under baseline
       alignment that slack is part of the flex line's descent, so centring
       the line put the visible text 10.5px from the masthead's top edge and
       left 31.5px of nothing under it — the row read as glued to the top,
       which is the regression this fixes.

       17 + 7, not 12 + 12. Splitting the slack evenly is the tempting
       font-independent answer and it measures 16.5 / 25.5: better, still
       visibly top-heavy, because the WORDMARK's own box leaves more room
       under its ink than over it. 17 / 7 is where the masthead's ink comes
       out symmetric, measured across every single-row width:

         현재     10.5 / 31.5      12 + 12    16.5 / 25.5
         17 + 7   21.0 / 21.0      (480px: 17.5 / 17.5)

       The pair is derived from measurement, so a typeface change can
       invalidate it. That is why browser.a11y.spec.ts asserts the SYMMETRY
       rather than these two numbers: if a new face moves the balance, the
       test fails and says by how much, instead of preserving a constant
       that has quietly stopped meaning anything.

       Sum is fixed at 24 by the 44px target (2 + 17 + 16 + 7 + 2), so
       changing one number means changing the other. Nothing else moves:
       height stays 44, the ::after underline stays on the padding box's
       bottom edge, and the wordmark-to-nav baseline difference stays 0. */
    padding-top: 17px;
    padding-bottom: 7px;
    /* The 2px rule is RESERVED SPACE, permanently transparent: the visible
       bar is the ::after below. Keeping the border means the row's height is
       identical in every state, so neither hovering nor landing on a page
       can shift the masthead by 2px (CLS stays 0).

       THE MATCHING TOP BORDER PREDATES BASELINE ALIGNMENT AND STILL EARNS
       ITS KEEP (2026-09-08 re-check). It was added when this rule read
       `align-items: center`, to stop the bottom border's 2px from being
       taken out of one side only and biasing the centred label 1px up. That
       specific bug cannot recur now that the cross-axis position comes from
       `align-items: baseline` above (baseline placement does not consult
       `min-height`'s slack the way centring did), so the border's role
       today is narrower but still real: it is what makes `.nav-link`'s
       border-box — hence the row height flex-baseline maths against — the
       same 44px whether or not the pseudo-element bar is visible, which is
       the CLS guarantee re-verified below. Removing it would not un-align
       the text, but it would let hover/current states change the element's
       geometry, which is the thing this rule exists to forbid.

       WHY THE TOP BORDER AND NOT DELETING THE BOTTOM ONE, which measures the
       same: ::after is positioned against the PADDING box, and the padding
       box's bottom edge is where the underline hangs from. Adding a top
       border takes its 2px off the top and leaves that edge exactly where it
       was, so the hover rule does not move. Removing the bottom border would
       have grown the padding box downward and taken the underline 2px with
       it. Height stays 44px in both cases (border-box), so the touch target
       is untouched either way. */
    border-block: 2px solid transparent;
    /* The bar is positioned against the padding box, which the transparent
       border sits just outside of — hence bottom: -2px below. */
    position: relative;
    transition: color var(--t-micro);
  }
  /* Hover underline, drawn from the CENTRE OUT. scaleX on a pseudo-element
     rather than an animated width/border: a transform is composited, so the
     bar cannot reflow the item it belongs to, and `transform-origin: center`
     is the whole of the "opens outwards from the middle" effect. The element
     is always in the box at scaleX(0) — nothing is created on hover, so
     there is nothing to lay out. */
  .nav-link::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: -2px;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--line-strong);
    transform: scaleX(0);
    transform-origin: center;
    transition: transform var(--t-micro);
  }
  .nav-link:hover::after {
    transform: scaleX(1);
  }
  /* Current page: THREE channels, unchanged in substance — ink colour,
     weight 800, and the bar — so the state never rests on colour alone
     (WCAG 1.4.1). The bar moved from border-bottom-color onto the same
     pseudo-element the hover uses, because two bars stacked in the same 2px
     of space would have hidden the accent under the hover grey. Declared
     AFTER the hover rule (equal specificity) so hovering the current item
     keeps the accent rather than repainting it. */
  .nav-link.current {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 800;
  }
  .nav-link.current::after {
    background: var(--accent);
    transform: scaleX(1);
  }
  /* Reduced motion: the underline still APPEARS — removing the state
     indicator would be a worse answer than removing the animation — it just
     arrives at full width instead of growing to it. The global rule in
     global.css already collapses the duration; this states the intent
     locally so a later edit to that rule cannot silently reintroduce the
     sweep here. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .nav-link::after {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
  /* TWO ROWS BELOW 406px, AND THE WRAP IS NOW DECLARED RATHER THAN EMERGENT
     (2026-09-10). `flex-wrap: wrap` alone left the masthead in one of two
     shapes with no way to tell them apart in CSS, and the two-row shape had
     never been styled — or measured. It showed, on a phone: the wordmark's
     box sat at y=0 with NOTHING above it, the two rows were 41px apart
     measured ink-to-ink, and the bar came to 101px tall at 390px.

     Why nothing above it: `min-height` is 57px and the wrapped content is
     100px, so `align-content: center` had no free space to distribute and
     every value that would have made room lived on the single-row path.
     Why 41px between: 8 (wordmark's own padding) + 16 (row-gap) + 17
     (.nav-link's top padding). That 17 is the SINGLE-ROW number — it exists
     to balance the nav's ink against the wordmark's across a SHARED
     baseline (see the long note above), and on separate rows there is no
     shared baseline to balance, so it is just 17px of dead space stacked on
     top of 16px more.

     `flex-basis: 100%` on the wordmark forces the wrap instead of waiting
     for one, so this block and the state it describes are the same thing.

     405, NOT A ROUND NUMBER: it is where the row stops fitting, measured.
     The wordmark is 102.6px and the nav 246.8px at the mobile tracking set
     below, so one row needs 102.6 + 16 + 246.8 = 365.4px of content — and
     `--gutter` is pinned at its 20px floor for every viewport under ~673px,
     so that is a 405.4px viewport. Rounding this UP to a comfortable 460
     was the first attempt and it is the wrong direction: 414, 428 and 430
     are all real phone widths that fit on one row today, and a tidier
     breakpoint would have taken a 58px bar to 91px on every one of them.

     Above 405 `flex-wrap: wrap` stays on .masthead-inner as the safety
     valve it always was — a face that renders wider than the subset wraps
     rather than overflowing 360px. If that ever fires it lands OUTSIDE this
     block and gets the single-row spacing on a two-row layout, which is the
     defect this pass fixed. That is deliberately not guarded with
     `flex-wrap: nowrap` (which would trade a spacing bug for a horizontal
     scrollbar); it is guarded by the e2e test instead, which asserts that
     the masthead's ink is symmetric in WHICHEVER state each width is in and
     so does not depend on this 405 staying accurate.

     The measured result, at 360px and 390px:
       위 잉크 15 · 로고~메뉴 22 · 아래 잉크 16 · 전체 93px  (was 8 / 41 / 15, 101px)
     Symmetric top and bottom, and the gap between the rows is now smaller
     than the rows' own height. The wrapped widths browser.a11y.spec.ts used
     to skip outright are the reason this shipped unseen. */
  @media (max-width: 405px) {
    .masthead-inner {
      /* 8/0, not a symmetric pair: the nav's own 12px padding and 2px
         border already supply the space under its ink, so the bar only
         needs padding on the side the wordmark is on to come out even.
         8 rather than the 6 the box arithmetic predicts — measured, the
         wordmark's line box starts 2px above its glyphs and the ink came
         out 13/16. At 8 it is 15/16. */
      padding-block: 8px 0;
      row-gap: 0;
    }
    .wordmark {
      flex-basis: 100%;
    }
    /* 12/12 — symmetric, because on its own row there is no wordmark
       baseline to sit against. Still 44px (2 + 12 + 16 + 12 + 2), so the
       touch target the e2e suite pins at 360px is untouched. */
    .nav-link {
      padding-top: 12px;
      padding-bottom: 12px;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .masthead-inner {
      min-height: var(--masthead-h-mobile);
    }
    /* FOUR ITEMS, ONE LINE, DOWN TO 360px. The trade below was measured and
       written for FIVE items (2026-09-07); with Artists cut from the nav
       (2026-09-08 — see the intro) the row is one item and one gap narrower
       at every width than the 312.6px this trade was sized for, so the
       margin this paragraph quotes (7.4px to spare, at 360px) only grows —
       every number recorded here is still a valid, now-conservative, upper
       bound on how tight the row is. It was measured for the old five-item
       row rather than re-measured for four because the fix this pass shipped
       (browser.a11y.spec.ts's mobile masthead test) does that measurement on
       every run; a comment cannot go stale the way a once-off number can. The
       trade is left in place rather than relaxed — loosening tracking/gap
       back toward the desktop values now that there is slack was not asked
       for and is a separate, and reversible, decision.
       ORIGINAL TRADE, MEASURED at 360px with five items (content 320px, nav
       on its own row under the wordmark). The row was 393.2px wide at the
       desktop values:
         · tracking 0.08em → 0.04em  −16.6px   (0.04em is the value .date and
           the footer's © already use, so no new number entered the system)
         · gap 24px → 8px            −64px
         = 312.6px, which fit with 7.4px to spare.
       WHAT WAS NOT TRADED. The type SIZE is untouched — a nav item is the
       same object here as at 1440, and shrinking it would have been the
       cheapest 20px and the most visible loss. Touch targets stayed whole:
       the narrowest item (NOTES) measured 45.4 x 44, so every item cleared
       44px in BOTH axes, not just the padded height — re-verified for the
       four-item row by the same e2e test.
       `flex-wrap: wrap` stays on the list above and is the point: below the
       wrap floor the row wraps again rather than overflowing, so the 360px
       horizontal-scroll contract holds by construction and a font that
       renders wider than the subset degrades into a second line instead of a
       scrollbar. */
    .nav-list {
      gap: var(--s-8);
    }
    .nav-link {
      letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    }
  }
</style>
